  <h2>6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>Recent Trends (2020s)</h3>
    <p>The 2020s have so far been defined by extreme whiplash: the COVID-19 collapse to $21.04 (April 2020), the post-pandemic recovery and Russia-Ukraine supply shock to $116.80 (June 2022), and a subsequent normalization toward the $60–$90 band in 2024–2026. The most recent data shows prices at <strong>$87.84 in March 2026</strong>, following a sharp $23.37 single-month increase from February, which ranks as the second-largest absolute monthly move in the dataset.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ol style="margin: 12px 0 0 24px;">
      <li><strong>Structural regime changes dominate.</strong> Crude oil does not oscillate around a stable mean — it transitions between distinct pricing regimes driven by supply-side structural factors (OPEC policy, shale technology, geopolitics). Mean reversion operates within regimes, not across them.</li>
      <li><strong>Asymmetric shock dynamics.</strong> The data shows that upward price shocks (embargoes, wars, supply disruptions) tend to be sharper in percentage terms but shorter in duration, while downward corrections (demand destruction, oversupply) tend to be larger in absolute dollar terms and more prolonged.</li>
      <li><strong>Volatility clusters.</strong> The 1970s, 2008–2009, and 2020–2022 represent distinct volatility clusters. Periods of low volatility (1990s, mid-2010s) tend to precede major dislocations, consistent with the Minsky hypothesis applied to commodity markets.</li>
      <li><strong>The $60–$90 band as a new anchor.</strong> Post-2022 normalization and the current 2025–2026 data suggest the market may be establishing a new equilibrium range of $60–$90/bbl — lower than the 2010s average but consistent with marginal production costs from non-OPEC sources.</li>
      <li><strong>Tail risk remains elevated.</strong> With a coefficient of variation of {s['std_dev']/s['mean']*100:.0f}% and a maximum single-month move of 217%, crude oil remains one of the most volatile major commodities. Analysts should maintain scenario frameworks that accommodate ±50% moves within a single year.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Methodological Note</h3>
    <p>All statistics, charts, and figures in this report are derived directly from the underlying dataset of 675 monthly observations. Prices are in nominal USD per barrel; no inflation adjustment has been applied. Some late-period values (2025–2026) may represent forecasts or projections rather than realized market prices. The CAGR and volatility metrics are computed on the full series and may mask significant within-period variation.</p>
