  <div class="card">
    <h3>Recent Trends (2020s)</h3>
    <p>The 2020s have so far been defined by extreme whiplash: the COVID-19 collapse to $21.04 (April 2020), the post-pandemic recovery and Russia-Ukraine supply shock to $116.80 (June 2022), and a subsequent normalization toward the $60&ndash;$90 band in 2024&ndash;2026. The most recent data shows prices at <strong>$87.84 in March 2026</strong>, following a sharp $23.37 single-month increase from February, which ranks as the second-largest absolute monthly move in the dataset.</p>

    <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
    <ol style="margin: 12px 0 0 24px;">
      <li><strong>Structural regime changes dominate.</strong> Crude oil does not oscillate around a stable mean — it transitions between distinct pricing regimes driven by supply-side structural factors (OPEC policy, shale technology, geopolitics). Mean reversion operates within regimes, not across them.</li>
      <li><strong>Asymmetric shock dynamics.</strong> The data shows that upward price shocks (embargoes, wars, supply disruptions) tend to be sharper in percentage terms but shorter in duration, while downward corrections (demand destruction, oversupply) tend to be larger in absolute dollar terms and more prolonged.</li>
      <li><strong>Volatility clusters.</strong> The 1970s, 2008&ndash;2009, and 2020&ndash;2022 represent distinct volatility clusters. Periods of low volatility (1990s, mid-2010s) tend to precede major dislocations, consistent with the Minsky hypothesis applied to commodity markets.</li>
